Task 0. Basics of HTTP/HTTPS.

Part 1. Differences between http and https:
- http (HyperText Transfer Protocol) transfers data in plain text over the internet.
- https (HTTP Secure) adds an encryption layer using SSL/TLS.

The key differences:
Encryption:
- http data is readable by anyone on the network.
- https encrypts the data so it looks like garbage to an eavesdropper.

Port:
- http defaults to port 80.
- https defaults to port 443.

Trust:
- http implies zero trust.
- https uses certificates (PKI) to verify the server is who they say they are.


Part 2: Structure of an HTTP Request and Response

Request Structure:
Start Line: METHOD + Path + HTTP Version (e.g., GET /login HTTP/1.1)
Headers: Key-value pairs providing metadata (e.g., User-Agent: Chrome, Host: google.com).
Body: (Optional) Data sent by the client (e.g., username=admin&password=123).

Example:
GET / HTTP/1.1
Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,image/avif,image/webp,image/apng,*/*;q=0.8,application/signed-exchange;v=b3;q=0.7
Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br, zstd
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9,az;q=0.8
Cache-Control: max-age=0
Connection: keep-alive
Cookie: cookiesession1=678A8C35E02A1D1A3CCFC25A908C74DC
DNT: 1
Host: isb.az
Sec-Fetch-Dest: document
Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate
Sec-Fetch-Site: none
Sec-Fetch-User: ?1
Upgrade-Insecure-Requests: 1
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/142.0.0.0 Safari/537.36
sec-ch-ua: "Chromium";v="142", "Google Chrome";v="142", "Not_A Brand";v="99"
sec-ch-ua-mobile: ?0
sec-ch-ua-platform: "Windows"

Response Structure:
Status Line: HTTP Version + Status Code + Message (e.g., HTTP/1.1 200 OK)
Headers: Metadata from server (e.g., Server: Apache, Content-Type: text/html).
Body: The content requested (e.g., The HTML code of the webpage).

Example:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Date: Sat, 06 Dec 2025 10:42:49 GMT
Server: Apache
X-Powered-By: PHP/7.4.33
Link: <https://isb.az/wp-json/>; rel="https://api.w.org/"
Cache-Control: max-age=31536000
Expires: Sun, 06 Dec 2026 10:42:49 GMT
Vary: Accept-Encoding
Content-Encoding: gzip
Connection: close
Content-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8
Content-Length: 15818

Part 3: Common HTTP Methods

3.1 Common HTTP Methods.

GET
Description: Requests a representation of a resource. No data should be changed on the server.
Use Case: Loading the homepage of a news site.

POST
Description: Submits data to be processed to a specified resource.
Use Case: Submitting a "Contact Us" form or logging into a bank account.

PUT
Description: Uploads a representation of a specific resource (updates/replaces it).
Use Case: Updating your profile bio via an API.

DELETE
Description: Deletes the specified resource.
Use Case: Deleting a photo from your cloud storage.

3.2 Common HTTP Status Codes

200 OK
Description: The request succeeded.
Scenario: You type google.com and the page loads perfectly.

301 Moved Permanently
Description: The resource has moved to a new URL.
Scenario: You visit http://mysite.com and are automatically redirected to https://mysite.com.

403 Forbidden
Description: The server understands the request but refuses to authorize it.
Scenario: A student tries to access the /admin panel of the school website without credentials.

404 Not Found
Description: The server cannot find the requested resource.
Scenario: You click a broken link or type a URL that doesn't exist.

500 Internal Server Error
Description: The server encountered an unexpected condition.
Scenario: A developer makes a typo in the website's code, causing it to crash when you try to load it.